# XBD 6.2 Character Encoding
# Copyright (c) 2026 Nicholas Martin. All rights reserved.
#
# Locale-enumerating tests take names from `locale -a`; the POSIX locale is
# always included.

assertion  XBD.6.2-character-encoding.001
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap06.html#6.2
class      required
since      1988
says       The POSIX locale has 256 single-byte characters: MB_CUR_MAX is 1,
           every byte value decodes, the 256 characters have distinct codes
           and each encodes back to its byte.
test       t/001-posix-256.c

assertion  XBD.6.2-character-encoding.002
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap06.html#6.2
class      required
since      1988
says       In every locale the portable characters leave the shift state
           initial after decoding or encoding.
test       t/002-initial-state.c

assertion  XBD.6.2-character-encoding.003
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap06.html#6.2
class      required
since      1988
says       The zero byte is the null character whatever the shift state, and
           never part of another character: after a byte that starts a
           longer character it ends the sequence as an error or stands
           alone.
test       t/003-zero-byte.c

assertion  XBD.6.2-character-encoding.004
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap06.html#6.2
class      disputed
since      1988
says       The bytes of period, slash, newline and carriage-return are never
           part of another character in any locale.
note       disputed (Q-208): the text does not say that the four bytes keep
           their meaning outside the initial shift state, so a locale with
           shift states is examined in the initial state only; a FAIL is
           logged as INSPECT.
test       t/004-four-bytes-alone.c

assertion  XBD.6.2-character-encoding.005
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap06.html#6.2
class      required
since      1988
says       In every locale MB_CUR_MAX is at least 1 and at most MB_LEN_MAX,
           and no character decoded or encoded is longer than MB_CUR_MAX
           bytes.
test       t/005-mb-cur-max.c
